de","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"מקרח","en":"from ice","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"צדק","en":"Jupiter","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"כוכב","en":"planet","alt":["star","the planet"],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"הלכת","en":"the planet","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"הגדול","en":"biggest","alt":["the big (sg. masc.)","the greatest"],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"במערכת","en":"in the solar system","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"הלוויין","en":"the satellite","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"הישראלי","en":"the israeli","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"הגיע","en":"arrived","alt":["(he) arrived","(he) has arrived"],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"לאתר","en":"to the site","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"השיגור","en":"the launch","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"החללית","en":"the spaceship","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"נכנסת","en":"enters","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"למסלול","en":"to the path","alt":["to the orbit","to the trajectory"],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"הסופי","en":"the final (sg. masc.)","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"הוא","en":"he","alt":["(he) is","(it) is"],"from":"phrase"},{"he":"ביותר","en":"very","alt":["biggest","the greatest","most"],"from":"phrase"},{"he":"השמש","en":"the sun","alt":["in the solar system","the sun is"],"from":"phrase"},{"he":"שלנו","en":"our","alt":["ours"],"from":"phrase"},{"he":"שלה","en":"its","alt":["her","hers"],"from":"phrase"}],"lexemes":["מטאור","חור שחור","לצדק","במסלול","שבתאי","בשביל החלב","השיגור","במכתשים","גשושית","למכתש","שביל החלב","ושבתאי","צדק","לשבתאי","לשיגור","מעבורות","המעבורת","שיגור","ליקוי","המכתש","הגשושית"],"tips":"**כוכב חמה**\n\nYou may see כוכב חמה and at first think \"why does חמה (hot in its feminine form) not agree with כוכב, which is a masculine noun?\". חמה is actually a fancy synonym for שמש (sun). So in fact כוכב חמה is \"sun-planet\": Mercury. *Remember that כוכב can mean star or planet, unless specified as a כוכב לכת, which is a planet.*\n\n---------------------------------------\n**The planets**\n\nThe planets, up to and including Saturn, each have their own Hebrew name in addition to the internationally recognised names. For example, Jupiter is צדק, but can also be referred to as יופיטר. Uranus and any other planets, dwarf planets and asteroids found in more modern times have a name based on the international names. Neptune, for example, is נפטון. However, on the 30th of December 2009, the Academy of the Hebrew Language announced Hebrew names for Uranus (אורון - Oron) and Neptune (רהב - Rahav). Only time will tell if these names catch on."}